[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'MiniMax H3’s most underrated capability may not be text-to-video or image-to-video generation at all It might actually be video editing All we need is a single reference image of a character and an ordinary video and we can completely replace the person in that video while preserving as much of the original action'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera movement and environment as possible Let’s start with the simplest example The person in the original video has been completely replaced with a new character with short blonde hair and a dark blue cheongsam Her walking pace, arm movements'

[00:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the movement of the camera and the surrounding environment all remain basically consistent with the original video Of course if it could only handle a simple replacement like this that still wouldn’t prove it has real video-editing capabilities'

[00:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So let’s increase the difficulty For example the character can film herself while brushing back her hair and making hand gestures which introduces some relatively complex movements'

[00:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We can then make it even harder by asking her to perform four key actions in sequence: applying blush putting on lipstick putting on glasses, and waving As you can see'

[01:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the overall generation quality is very high We’ve replaced the character while preserving the environment and the original subject’s actions And that’s only the beginning In this video we’ll fully test character replacement complex actions'

[01:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'complex facial expressions prop replacement and clothing replacement This time we’re once again using the 10Eros Max model which I introduced in the previous video This is not a LoRA'

[01:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It is a complete Ref2VA base model Since Turbo acceleration is already integrated into the model we don’t need to add another acceleration LoRA after it To balance generation speed with final image clarity'

[01:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we’re still using a two-stage sampling workflow You’ll need to install a few extensions The first one is MiniMax H3 Latent Upscaler Simply clone it into your custom_nodes folder'

[01:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then download the corresponding model Next let’s break down the workflow To make the demonstration easier I’ve also built this workflow on RunningHub In the ComfyUI ecosystem'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'RunningHub is an excellent online workspace because whenever a new model or extension appears the platform usually adds support for it very quickly RunningHub also gives ComfyUI creators an online community where you can find plenty of creative inspiration and example workflows The entire workflow can be divided into six sections'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let’s begin with the shared configuration I’ve separated the aspect ratio and duration into their own inputs You only need to choose whether you want a landscape or portrait video and then set the total duration'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'From those settings we can determine the video dimensions and total frame count For this example I’ve set the duration to five seconds which works out to roughly one hundred twenty-four frames If we use a portrait format like the one I’m using here'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the aspect ratio is approximately three-to-four During the first sampling stage we set the megapixel value to zero point two There’s an important detail here Notice that we don’t need to resize the reference image because it only provides visual features for the character'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prop or clothing The reference video, however does need to be resized Since we’re performing video editing'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the dimensions of the reference video should match the width and height of the final output You also need to pay attention to the frame rate of the original video Different source videos may use twenty-four thirty'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or sixty frames per second so before we begin editing it’s best to standardize that baseline first After the base model we connect the special attention mechanism I mentioned earlier and then move into the first sampling stage Next'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we upscale the result with our three-dimensional latent upscaling model before continuing with the second sampling stage Finally we get the video we want In this example I’m mainly performing an outfit replacement The woman’s original clothing has been replaced with the outfit I specified'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and as you can see the result looks excellent Now let’s talk about how to write the prompt A large part of the final video-editing quality depends on how your prompt is written MiniMax has already provided an official prompt-writing guide'

[04:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'along with an H3 Prompt Writing Skill You can install this Skill in Codex Claude Code or another AI agent and have it write the prompt for you directly The official Ref2VA prompt contains six sections'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The first is called subject definitions This is where we clearly define the exact role of each reference asset The second section is the summary where we explain the entire editing task in a single sentence'

[04:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For example we might say that we only want to replace the white dress and shoes in the original video The third section specifies which information needs to be preserved The fourth section is the detailed description'

[05:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which usually provides a precise description of the actions in the video Depending on the situation you can keep the action description fairly simple because the reference video already contains a complete demonstration of the movement Sometimes, however'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you need to describe those actions much more clearly We’ll see an example of that in a moment Now let’s run a complete set of tests Our first test is still the simplest one: replacing a character while preserving a basic action'

